WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:07.129
This is really funny.

00:00:07.129 --> 00:00:12.820
So last week when I was teasing the bonus
show, previewing the stories on the bonus

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:18.900
show, I had a verbal slip and I called it
the bonus show and we chose not to edit it

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:20.690
out because we don't edit the show.

00:00:20.690 --> 00:00:21.759
We actually do.

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:23.240
The show is just we just film it.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:24.240
And that's the show.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.850
And it was sort of funny.

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:31.289
And there were expectedly memes and there
were songs and remixes and all this different

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:32.289
stuff.

00:00:32.289 --> 00:00:37.739
But there are now lots of callers who are
casually referring to it as the bonus show

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:38.739
and voicemails.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:41.359
And that's not even like the main point of
the call.

00:00:41.359 --> 00:00:43.700
And I have one such call today about that.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:46.210
But this is actually really, really interesting.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:52.820
The other day and I took the New York Times
dialect quiz, I mentioned having friends from

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.010
the South who claim I have a New York accent.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:58.410
And sometimes people call into the show and
say, I have a Boston accent, but I don't have

00:00:58.410 --> 00:00:59.410
either.

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.120
I didn't grow up in Boston or New York and
I did this New York Times dialect quiz and

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.040
it came back saying, I have a southern western
New England accent primarily, which is accurate.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:11.140
That's exactly where I grew up.

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:17.591
And that I have elements of my dialect in
English that are typically from Yonkers, New

00:01:17.591 --> 00:01:20.280
York, and Pembroke Pines, Florida.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:27.960
Now, I hypothesized, I wonder if there is
some aspect of what is sometimes called the

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:33.290
Jewish American dialect that would be centered
around Yonkers and Pembroke Pines.

00:01:33.290 --> 00:01:34.570
That has kind of that.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.640
There are traces of it in my speech.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:42.710
And this caller, who, again, casually refers
to the bonus show as the bonus show, has a

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.810
different explanation for those elements of
my speech.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:46.810
Take a listen.

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.810
This is really interesting.

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:52.510
Hey, David, I was listening to your show the
other day, and you were talking about how

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:56.130
you took the quiz to determine your dialect.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:57.130
Yes.

00:01:57.130 --> 00:02:02.710
And I believe that one of the things it's
important to you to be having a dialog from

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:04.880
the Pembroke Pines, Florida area.

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:07.360
Well, I have an office in terms of times.

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:09.910
I'm familiar with the area.

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:13.720
And I believe you attributed that to perhaps
your Jewish heritage.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.971
But I would like to set the record straight.

00:02:16.971 --> 00:02:17.971
Please, please.

00:02:17.971 --> 00:02:23.060
Pembroke Pines is has a very high Hispanic
population, about over 40 percent Hispanic,

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:26.030
primarily college educated, mostly from South
America.

00:02:26.030 --> 00:02:31.490
So I believe that may be where that hint of
dialect may come from.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:34.750
So that is very, very interesting.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:42.450
And in fact, if you look at Pembroke Pines,
at Yonkers, New York, which is the other place

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:48.590
where my my accent has traces, has roots,
there's also a large Hispanic population in

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:49.590
Yonkers.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:50.810
So that may be accurate.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:53.590
And I was thinking back, you know, when I
first moved to the United States, I only knew

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.590
two words.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.580
I knew the word stop and I knew the word blue.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.970
And I remember leaving JFK Airport and seeing
a stop sign.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.350
What are the odds, you know, a five year old
me, what are the odds that right away I see

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:08.450
a word that I know this is going to go really,
really easily.

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:09.450
It's going to be great.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:10.980
And of course, it was difficult.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:16.770
But the early folks when I was in school,
they had, you know, sort of like helpers.

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.050
This is when schools, I think, were maybe
better funded and there was more staffing.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:25.870
But some of the other kids who were learning
English coming from Spanish speaking countries,

00:03:25.870 --> 00:03:30.600
there were sort of like helpers who spoke
English with accents.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:33.620
They were native Spanish speakers who spoke
English with accents.

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:39.870
And I wonder my early English exposure, other
than Mr. Rogers, was my parents who speak

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:47.050
English with an accent sourced from Spanish
language and the helpers at school who would

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.800
have that exact same accent in English, having
learned English as a second language.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.990
So I believe that this caller may very much
be on to something very, very much be on to

00:03:55.990 --> 00:03:56.990
something.

00:03:56.990 --> 00:03:57.990
OK, that's it.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.830
I went on for a long time, but that's the
story on today's bonus show.

